; 35-flat.kurt

; By default, a chain of the same infix operator nests two arguments at a
; time, left to right:

infix "+" 20 20
var a, b, c
parse a + b + c            ; (+ (+ a b) c)

; `flat OP` tells Kurt that `OP` doesn't need that nesting -- a run of `OP`s
; is really one operator applied to all of its arguments at once, which is
; how mathematicians usually think of `+`, `and`, `or`, etc.:
; This is fixed semantic behavior, so `flat` also declares `+` constant.

flat "+"
parse a + b + c            ; (+ a b c)

; `and` (04-and.kurt) is declared flat this same way -- in the core,
; `minimal.kurt` (`flat and`), which every file starts with, not in
; `propositional.kurt` -- which is why `A and B and C` reads naturally as one
; three-way conjunction rather than a conjunction of a conjunction.
